body:
When two or more workspace folders define an MCP server under the same name, `McpWorkbenchService.sort()` used a comparator that always returned `-1` for equal-name, equal-enablement entries. This non-antisymmetric comparator produces unstable, alternating permutations across repeated in-place `Array.prototype.sort` calls, so the "winning" server in `getEnabledLocalMcpServers()` flips every re-sort — triggering an unbounded loop of `Overwriting mcp server` warnings and re-registrations.

## Changes

- **`mcpWorkbenchService.ts`**: Replaced the tie-break logic in `sort()` so equal-name entries with equal enablement state fall back to a deterministic secondary key (`id`) instead of unconditionally returning `-1`. This makes the comparator antisymmetric and the sort order stable across repeated calls.
- **`mcpWorkbenchService.test.ts`**: Added a regression test that triggers repeated re-sorts (via `onDidChangeAllowedMcpServers`) on a set of same-named servers across `User`/`Workspace` scopes, asserting both `service.local` order and the `getEnabledLocalMcpServers()` winner remain stable.

```ts
// Before: always -1 for equal-name, equal-enablement entries (non-antisymmetric)
if (!a.runtimeStatus || a.runtimeStatus.state === Enabled) { return -1; }
if (!b.runtimeStatus || b.runtimeStatus.state === Enabled) { return 1; }
return 0;

// After: deterministic tie-break
const aEnabled = !a.runtimeStatus || a.runtimeStatus.state === Enabled;
const bEnabled = !b.runtimeStatus || b.runtimeStatus.state === Enabled;
if (aEnabled !== bEnabled) { return aEnabled ? -1 : 1; }
return a.id.localeCompare(b.id);
```

